# Integration, connectivity and app settings

Keep a discovered device, an integration entry, an app and a dashboard card as separate things, with their own names and outcomes. An integration connects Home Assistant to a service or device. An app is software managed by an installation that supports apps.

This page covers the management routes registered by Home Assistant frontend **20261028.5**, used by Core **2026.11.1**. It gives design ownership to the routes in the [interface coverage register](interface-coverage.html). It does not replace native integration forms, supply credentials or install software.

<h2 id="integration-capabilities">Use the actual management contract</h2>

| Surface | Show | Keep |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Discovery and integration setup | The identity and source that are available, and what setup would connect | The integration’s native steps, options, permission checks and cancellation |
| Credentials and reauthentication | Which integration needs attention and the reported authentication problem | Native credential entry, secret masking, validation and authorised recovery |
| Connectivity and protocol panels | The chosen adapter or network, its devices, observations and permitted operations | The protocol’s own units, identifiers, pairing and reset consequences and support limits |
| Apps and marketplace | Installed or available software, version, source and reported status | Installation-type restrictions, native update and install confirmation, and permissions |
| Cloud and remote connection | Which connection is being set up and its reported state | Account scope, native sign-in and a plain explanation of remote access |

The pinned settings router includes entry points for Bluetooth, DHCP, infrared, LoRaWAN, Matter, Modbus, MQTT, radio frequency, serial, SSDP, Thread, Zeroconf, ZHA and Z-Wave JS. A route in the source does not mean it is enabled in every installation. The KNX and Insteon links can open panels that the integration owns. Apply the shared layout and state rules and keep each panel’s own controls. A protocol dashboard does not show that every device has the same capabilities.

<h2 id="integration-discovery">Discovery is a setup opportunity</h2>

Use a clear name and any source identifier that helps people tell devices apart. Do not call a discovery trusted, paired or ready until the relevant check has succeeded. Ignoring a discovery, removing an integration entry and deleting a device have different effects, so explain the chosen operation at its own confirmation step.

<%= example('admin-discovery') %>

<h2 id="integration-credentials">Keep credentials within their native flow</h2>

Show which connection needs reauthentication without putting a token in an error message, example, screenshot or copied diagnostic. Keep the person’s place and any non-secret draft when the native flow allows it. If the backend outcome is not known, say the result has not been confirmed. Do not present a temporary timeout as a rejected password, and do not let a local visual control approve an account.

<%= example('admin-credential-status') %>

<h2 id="integration-protocols">Make protocol observations interpretable</h2>

Keep a device’s last observation, an adapter’s connection and an application’s overall availability apart. Name the protocol’s measure and its unit. A count of discovered devices is not a count of working devices. A missing observation stays unknown, and a signal-strength value alone is not a health score. Keep the native pairing, inclusion, interview, configuration and reset wording where it matters to the task.

<%= example('admin-protocol-diagnostics') %>

<h2 id="integration-apps">Keep app lifecycle and automation state distinct</h2>

Write “app” and use the native **Apps** label when you direct people to the interface. Add “previously add-on” once where it helps people reading older instructions. An installed app is not necessarily running, and a running app does not mean its connected devices work. Keep the reported state apart from a start, stop or update request. “Start on boot” is a setting, not an instruction to start now.

<%= example('admin-app-state') %>

<h2 id="integration-presentation">Shared appearance and accessible interaction</h2>

Use the [card](../components/cards.html), [summary list](../components/summary-list.html), [form](../components/controls.html), [error](../content/writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html) and [dialog](../components/dialogs.html) patterns. Keep protocol icons that do a job and give each a text name, not an unexplained decoration. Show the chosen network and the action target in text, not only in colour.

Keep keyboard access through nested configuration flows, return focus when the person cancels, and keep validation beside its field. A long hardware identifier may wrap on screen without changing the value that is copied. Loading, permission denied, no results and disconnected are separate states. Do not replace a native protocol diagram with a table unless the table keeps the task working, and provide an accessible data or list view beside the diagram where you can.

<h2 id="integration-acceptance">Acceptance checks</h2>

1. Test supported and unsupported installation types, missing integrations, restricted accounts and a connection that disappears during setup.
2. Check discovery, configure, reauthenticate, disable, remove and delete separately. Each label must describe the real backend operation and its target.
3. Test an authentication failure, a cancellation, a timeout and a late response without showing a secret or reporting an unconfirmed success.
4. Check protocol identifiers, numeric units, selection and confirmations. Test keyboard navigation, 320px reflow, enlarged text and forced colours.
5. Check the installed, running, updating and stopped states of an app against the real responses. Keep the version choice, the update request and its completion apart.

The examples use invented records and local feedback. Native installation and device acceptance needs evidence for the real Home Assistant version, integration and account. Follow [Action requests and results](action-results.html), [Accounts, permissions and shared settings](accounts-and-permissions.html), [Connection loss and recovery](connection-and-recovery.html) and the [Compatibility register and native fallback](compatibility-register.html).

